    "vector_context": {
      "decision_trace": [
        "init_responder_session_from_pinned_secrets",
        "decrypt_header_with_current_or_next_key",
        "advance_symmetric_chain_within_epoch",
        "dh_ratchet_on_new_header_key",
        "recover_inner_rumors_in_order"
      ],
      "notes": "Generated by driving nostr-double-ratchet@0.0.138 (irislib, MIT; the production iris wire library) with the pinned session-setup secrets under a deterministic RNG (label heterodyne-dm-transcript-v1) and a Date.now() counter starting at (test_epoch+200)s advancing 1s per call, so the suite regenerates it byte-for-byte. Conformance is receive-side: replaying the outer events in delivery order through both sessions must yield these inner rumors. input.ratchet_keys pins the DH ratchet keys each party generated during the transcript (epoch 1 = alice's Session.init draw / bob's first-receive ratchet, epoch 2 = each party's next ratchet); implementations under test inject them where they would otherwise draw fresh ratchet keys - the standard Double Ratchet test-vector seam - because post-ratchet messages are undecryptable without them. Each pinned public_key equals the nextPublicKey advertised in that party's prior-chain headers and (for epoch 1) the outer signer of that party's post-ratchet messages. The outer signer is the sender's CURRENT ratchet key, rotated per DH ratchet step, NOT per message: seq 0 and 1 share alice's epoch-0 signer (her session pubkey), seq 3 uses her epoch-1 signer. Relays therefore cannot link outer events to a persona, but CAN link same-epoch messages to each other (5.7.1, 9.5). Inner rumors are unsigned kind:14 with id, pubkey = the sender's device publishing key bound to the persona via kind:31001 (5.7.2)."
    }
